      "analysis": "The actual reduced differential maps ker(d)-orthogonal bijectively onto its actual range; closedness makes its inverse continuous, and exactness identifies that range with the next kernel. Finite dimensionality is used only to establish the required closed ranges. The simultaneous finite_reduced_control construction ties the same s and G to both endpoint zeros, every contraction equation, both inverse equations, norm(s) bounded by the actual reduced inverse norm, norm(G) bounded by the adjacent max squared, and the corresponding energy bound. The explicit H(n+1)=task H_n shift and two zero endpoint spaces cover every original degree without imposing identities on the unused tail.",
